Compare and contrast the surrender value and the paid-up value as two options available to a policyholder who can no longer afford to pay premiums on a 15-year endowment policy that has been in force for 6 years. In your answer:
(a) define each concept and explain how each is calculated;
(b) describe the financial implications for the policyholder under each option, with reference to the illustrative surrender value calculation in this chapter;
(c) explain the NIIRA 2025 obligations on the insurer to communicate these options to the policyholder; and (d) explain the actuarial impact on the insurer’s reserve when a policyholder elects paid-up status.

NIIRA 2025’s introduction of a Risk-Based Capital (RBC) solvency framework represents a fundamental change in the capital adequacy regime for Nigerian life insurers. Critically evaluate the implications of this change for:
(a) the actuarial function, including the additional modelling requirements and the Statutory Actuary’s expanded reporting obligations;
(b) the Board and senior management of a life insurance company, with reference to the RBC components (C1 through C4) and their management;
(c) the connection between the RBC framework and the mandatory IFRS 17 measurement regime; and (d) the implications for smaller Nigerian life insurers that may lack the actuarial capacity to implement a full RBC assessment internally.
